{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli Summary**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was a vassal of the County of Edessa until it fell to the Zengids in 1144 then transferred his allegiance to the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states. He was involved in the succession disputes of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1174 he supported Miles of Plancy against Amalric of Lusignan for the regency of Baldwin IV. After Miles' assassination Raymond became sole regent in 1175. He ruled wisely and stabilized the kingdom during Baldwin IV's minority. Raymond III also mediated conflicts between factions of the nobility.\n\nIn 1185 Raymond III helped secure the succession of Baldwin V. He was part of the delegation that invited Baldwin V's maternal grandfather William V of Montferrat to be his guardian. Raymond III also negotiated the truce with Saladin in 1185 which held for two years. This truce allowed Raymond III to focus on internal stability in Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without a male heir. His death came as a blow to the Crusader states. His passing marked the end of an era of relative stability. Raymond III's death also came at a critical juncture with Saladin's forces on the move. His successor Raymond IV of Tripoli was his namesake and cousin but lacked his political acumen.\n\nRaymond III's legacy includes his efforts to maintain unity among the Crusader states. His regency in Jerusalem is remembered as a period of relative peace and stability. Raymond III's diplomatic skills were crucial 